    Besides the equation given above for an ellipse, it can also be represented parametrically,
    with $\phi$ the angle drawn in Figure \ref{fig:ellipse}:

    \[x = a \cos \theta,\ y = b \sin \theta,\ \theta \in [0, 2 \pi)\]

% ===================================================================================================
\section{A Problem to Solve with an Ellipse:  Arc Length and Inverse}

    One sees online comments, e.g. \cite{SE01}, about elliptic integrals and functions
    deriving from arc length problems, but actual examples are hard to find.
    Most of the applications for elliptic integrals
    and functions go back to problems from specialized differential equations or engineering
    problems.

    I persisted in this question and found answers that tie back to the functions
    in the C47/R47 calculator.  Here are my findings.

    First, there is the problem of notation and parameter names.
    This is discussed in \cite{jdcook01}.  An elliptic function like
    $sn(u,m)$ can be described with either $m$ or $k$ given as the second argument, and
    sometimes the second argument is not mentioned at all.

    Similarly, an elliptic integral can be described as $K(m)$ or $F(\phi,k)$ or $F(\phi,m)$,
    with apparently $k$ and $m$ being different things.  And then, their relationship to
    geometric parameters that we know from simple conic descriptions, like $a$ or $b$ or
    $e$, is not obvious.

    The simple answer is, that when you see $k$ as a parameter, it is called the ``modulus'',
    but actually it is equal to the eccentricity $e$ as seen in elementary conic section
    terminology.  Some sources instead refer to $m$ as the modulus.

    However, though $k$ is often favoured in mathematical treatments, you often
    see in software such as the C47/R47 or Mathematica a parameter called $m$.  Actually, this is
    almost the same thing:  $m=k^2=e^2$.  This $m$ parameter is just the square of
    eccentricity.

    Now, a typical second year calculus textbook for engineering students teaches how to
    calculate arc length in two- or three- dimensional Euclidean space, using integration.
    The Olmsted book, \cite{Olmsted01}, does a good job of this.

    To paraphrase \cite{Olmsted01}:
    If $C$ is an arc with parametrization functions $x = f(t), y = g(t)$, with continuous
    derivatives on $[a,b]$, then $C$ is rectifiable with length

    \[L = \int_{a}^{b}{\sqrt{(f'(t))^2+(g'(t))^2}\, d t}\]

    As an example of this, for the ellipse described above, we get (with a little
    rearrangement):

    The total arc length of the ellipse is \cite{Olmsted01}

    \[4 a \int_{0}^{\pi / 2}{\sqrt{1 - k^2 \sin^2{\theta}}\, d \theta}\]

    This leads to the following.  Incomplete elliptic integral of the second kind:

    \[E(\phi,\mo{m}) = \int_{0}^{\phi}{\sqrt{1 - \mo{m} \sin^2{\theta}}\, d \theta}\]

    Complete elliptic integral of the second kind:

    \[E(\mo{m}) = \int_{0}^{\pi / 2}{\sqrt{1 - \mo{m} \sin^2{\theta}}\, d \theta}\]

    Confusingly, in different parts of the literature, you will see this version instead.
    Incomplete elliptic integral of the second kind:

    \[E(\phi,\mo{k}) = \int_{0}^{\phi}{\sqrt{1 - \mo{k}^2 \sin^2{\theta}}\, d \theta}\]

    Complete elliptic integral of the second kind:

    \[E(\mo{k}) = \int_{0}^{\pi / 2}{\sqrt{1 - \mo{k}^2 \sin^2{\theta}}\, d \theta}\]

    See also \cite{WI01}.

    Just understand that $k$ is eccentricity of the ellipse, $m=k^2$, and that
    for both you have the restriction $k \in [0,1), m \in [0,1)$.

    So now we have an understandable application if you followed all the bits about
    conic sections, eccentricity, and arc length.  We simply want to take an ellipse
    and calculate its arc length.  The incomplete elliptic integral of the
    second kind does this for arbitrary angle $\phi$, meaning we start at angle
    $0$ and rotate counter-clockwise to $\phi$, measuring arc length as we go.
    The complete elliptic integral of the second kind calculates
    the perimeter of the ellipse.

    Let us take an example and run it through both Mathematica and the C47/R47.

    Take $a=5, b=1$, so we have the ellipse $\frac{x^2}{5^2}+\frac{y^2}{1}=1$.

    We have eccentricity $k=e=\sqrt{1-\frac{b^2}{a^2}}=\sqrt{24/25}$.  Both Mathematica
    and the C47/R47 use a parameter named $m$, so we use modulus $m=.96$.

    \begin{tip}
    The calculator will do that step for you.  \gmkey{a,b$\rightarrow$m} on the blue row of
    \menu{Ellipt} takes the two semi-axes off the stack and returns the modulus directly, so the
    $m$ for this ellipse is \key{5}\quad\key{ENTER}\quad\key{1}\quad\gmkey{a,b$\rightarrow$m}.  It
    sorts the two axes itself, so the order you enter them in does not matter.
    \end{tip}

    In Mathematica, we can compute this:

\begin{lstlisting}
EllipticE[.96]

--> 1.05050
\end{lstlisting}

    As a sanity check, we can also compute:

\begin{lstlisting}
Integrate[Sqrt[1 - .96 (Sin[t])^2], {t, 0, Pi/2}]

--> 1.05050
\end{lstlisting}

    Also:

\begin{lstlisting}
ArcLength[{5 Sin[t], Cos[t]}, {t, 0, Pi/2}]

--> 5.25251
\end{lstlisting}

    which is $5$ times the value above, also confirming our correctness.

    If we want to calculate the partial arc length, say for an angle $\phi$ of $.2$ radians
    and the same modulus, we run

\begin{lstlisting}
EllipticE[.2, .96]

--> 0.19872
\end{lstlisting}

    On the C47/R47, we do this:

    \seq{\key{.}\key{9}\key{6}\quad\mkey{E(m)}}{X:\ \ 1.050 50}

    For the partial angle, in radians:

    \seq{\key{.}\key{9}\key{6}\quad\key{ENTER}\quad\key{.}\key{2}\quad\fmkey{E($\phi$,m)}}{X:\ \ 0.198 72}

    \shotpair{02-em}{03-ephim}{Left, the perimeter parameter: \mkey{E(m)} at $m=.96$ leaves
    \screen{1.050 50}, matching Mathematica.  Right, the partial arc at $\phi=.2$, which needs radian
    mode: the \reg{r} in the status bar has replaced the degree sign, and the answer is
    \screen{0.198 72}.  The menu stays open under the result, so the next function is one key away.}

    This confirms that elliptic integrals of the second kind give correct arc length
    calculations.

    \begin{note}
    The complete integral is on the menu's first row and the incomplete one is on the orange row
    above it, so \mkey{E(m)} is a plain soft key and \fmkey{E($\phi$,m)} takes a shift first.  The
    same split holds for the first kind, \mkey{K(m)} against \fmkey{F($\phi$,m)}.  The complete
    forms take one argument and the incomplete forms take two, $m$ in \reg{Y} and the angle in
    \reg{X}.
    \end{note}

    Now let us look at the inverse problem.  For a given modulus and a given arc length,
    we want to know what angle $\phi$ produces that arc length.

    Unfortunately, my researches show that there is no such function in either the mathematical literature or the
    software packages of Mathematica or C47/R47.  We have to resort to other methods such as root
    solving.  See \cite{SE03} for more on this issue.

    In Mathematica, try this:

\begin{lstlisting}
FindRoot[EllipticE[x, .96] == .19872, {x, .5}]

--> 0.20000
\end{lstlisting}

    Correct answer.

    \needspace{12\baselineskip}
    In the C47/R47, enter a program:

    \prog{0001: LBL 'EllE'\\0002: \ \ MVAR 'x'\\0003: \ \ MVAR 'm'\\0004: \ \ MVAR 'u'\\0005: \ \ RCL 'm'\\0006: \ \ RCL 'x'\\0007: \ \ E($\phi$,m)\\0008: \ \ RCL− 'u'\\0009: END}

    To key it in, one action per line:

    \begin{keying}
        \step{\key{XEQ}\,\key{.}\,\key{.}}{open a new program}
        \step{\fkey{PRGM}}{enter program-entry mode}
        \step{\gkey{LBL}\,\fkey{$\alpha$}\,\key{E}\,\key{l}\,\key{l}\,\key{E}\,\key{ENTER}}{the label \cmd{EllE}}
        \step{\mkey{P.FN1}\ \mkey{P.FN2}\ \mkey{MVAR}\,\fkey{$\alpha$}\,\key{x}\,\key{ENTER}}{declare the first variable; the menu stays on \menu{P.FN2}}
        \step{\mkey{MVAR}\,\fkey{$\alpha$}\,\key{m}\,\key{ENTER}}{the second variable}
        \step{\mkey{MVAR}\,\fkey{$\alpha$}\,\key{u}\,\key{ENTER}}{the third}
        \step{\key{RCL}\,\fkey{$\alpha$}\,\key{m}\,\key{ENTER}}{begin the body}
        \step{\key{RCL}\,\fkey{$\alpha$}\,\key{x}\,\key{ENTER}}{}
        \step{\gkey{X.FN}\,\key{$\uparrow$}\ \gmkey{Ellipt}\ \fmkey{E($\phi$,m)}}{the incomplete integral, from the second page of the extended-functions menu}
        \step{\key{RCL}\,\key{$-$}\,\fkey{$\alpha$}\,\key{u}\,\key{ENTER}}{}
        \step{\fkey{PRGM}}{leave program-entry mode}
    \end{keying}

    Application Note~29 \cite{AN0029} walks through program entry from the beginning if any of that
    is unfamiliar.

    \shot{04-elle}{\cmd{EllE} keyed in, seen from the top: nine steps in 38 bytes.  The listing
    continues past \cmd{RCL} \vr{x} with \fmkey{E($\phi$,m)} and \cmd{RCL$-$} \vr{u}, and the
    \menu{Ellipt} menu is still open underneath because the last function was taken from it.  The
    three \cmd{MVAR} declarations are what let the solver ask for $m$ and $u$ and solve for $x$.}

    \begin{note}
    \cmd{MVAR} is not on \fkey{ADV}, which is where most readers look for it.  In program-entry mode
    it is two pages into the program-functions menu already on the screen: \mkey{P.FN1} on soft key
    6, \mkey{P.FN2} on soft key 6 again, then \mkey{MVAR}.  Declaring a variable with \cmd{MVAR} is
    what lets the solver ask you for it afterwards.
    \end{note}

    If you run this program \cmd{EllE} under \fkey{ADV}, \mkey{SOLVE}
    with a modulus of $.96$ and a $u$ value of $.19872$, you will get an answer
    of $0.20000$.  Naming the program puts its three declared variables on the soft keys in the order
    they were declared, so \mkey{m} takes the modulus, \mkey{u} takes the arc length, and \mkey{x} is
    the one to solve for:

    \seq{\key{.}\key{9}\key{6}\ \ \mkey{m}\quad\key{.}\key{1}\key{9}\key{8}\key{7}\key{2}\ \
         \mkey{u}\quad\mkey{x}}{x:\ \ 0.200 00}

    \shot{05-solve}{The angle recovered, \screen{0.200 00}, with the star on the \mkey{x} soft key
    marking the variable that was solved for.  Run this one in degrees by mistake and it returns
    \screen{11.458 99}, which is the same answer in the other unit and no error at all.}

    This accounts for $E(m)$ and $E(\phi,m)$ on the C47/R47, which are the Elliptic Integrals of the Second Kind.

% ===================================================================================================
\section{Elliptic Integrals of the First Kind}

    The integrals of the first kind are $K(m)$ (complete) and $F(\phi, m)$ (incomplete).

    Equations:

    \[F(\phi, m) = \int_{0}^{\phi}{\frac{d \theta}{\sqrt{1-m \sin^2{\theta}}}}\]

    \[K(m) = \int_{0}^{\pi/2}{\frac{d \theta}{\sqrt{1-m \sin^2{\theta}}}}\]

    These also have a geometric interpretation, from Complex Analysis.  On constructing a conformal
    map from an ellipse to a unit circle, the elliptic integral of the first kind
    tells you the angular displacement needed
    to map arc length in the ellipse to the corresponding point in the circle \cite{SE02}.

    In this case, there is an inverse mapping.  $am(x)$, described in
    Section \ref{jacobiEllipFns}, is the inverse
    of the incomplete elliptic integral of the first kind.

% ===================================================================================================
\section{Elliptic Integrals of the Third Kind and Jacobi Zeta}

    $\Pi(n,m)$ is the incomplete Elliptic Integral of the third kind.  It has applications in Physics,
    e.g. electrostatic potential.

    In Mathematica, use \cmd{EllipticPi[n,m]}.  On the C47/R47, use \mkey{$\Pi$(n,m)}.

    Comparison of values from Mathematica and the C47/R47:

\begin{lstlisting}
EllipticPi[0.4,0.6]

--> 2.59092
\end{lstlisting}

    \seq{\key{.}\key{6}\quad\key{ENTER}\quad\key{.}\key{4}\quad\mkey{$\Pi$(n,m)}}{X:\ \ 2.590 92}

    The Jacobi Zeta function is found in Mathematica as \cmd{JacobiZeta[$\phi$,m]} and on
    the C47/R47 as \fmkey{Z($\phi$,m)}.  It has applications in physics and in interpretation of
    elliptic arc compared with circle.

    Mathematica example:

\begin{lstlisting}
JacobiZeta[2,.5]

--> -0.11777
\end{lstlisting}

    C47/R47 example:

    \seq{\key{.}\key{5}\quad\key{ENTER}\quad\key{2}\quad\fmkey{Z($\phi$,m)}}{X:\ \ -0.117 77}

    These two match.

