\section{Summary of C47/R47 Elliptic Functions}

    Table \ref{tab:summary} lists the C47/R47 functions this note has described, drawn as the soft
    keys that reach them on the single page of \menu{Ellipt}.  A plain key sits on the menu's first
    row; an orange one takes the f shift first, and a blue one the g shift.  The last three rows are
    the parameter conversions rather than elliptic functions, and they are on the page because that
    is where they are needed.  Figure \ref{fig:ellipt} is the page itself, for checking the table
    against.

    \begin{ntable}{@{}l l@{}}{The elliptic functions described here, and where each one sits on the
             single page of the Ellipt menu.}{tab:summary}
        \hd{Soft key} & \hd{Function} \\ \midrule
        \fmkey{am(u,m)}      & Jacobi Amplitude Function \\
        \mkey{sn(u,m)}       & Jacobi sn Function \\
        \mkey{cn(u,m)}       & Jacobi cn Function \\
        \mkey{dn(u,m)}       & Jacobi dn Function \\
        \mkey{K(m)}          & Complete Elliptic Integral of First Kind \\
        \fmkey{F($\phi$,m)}  & Incomplete Elliptic Integral of First Kind \\
        \mkey{E(m)}          & Complete Elliptic Integral of Second Kind \\
        \fmkey{E($\phi$,m)}  & Incomplete Elliptic Integral of Second Kind \\
        \mkey{$\Pi$(n,m)}    & Elliptic Integral of Third Kind \\
        \fmkey{Z($\phi$,m)}  & Jacobi Zeta \\
        \addlinespace
        \gmkey{a,b$\rightarrow$m} & Semi-axes to modulus, $m = 1 - (a/b)^2$ with $a$ the smaller \\
        \fmkey{k$\rightarrow$m}   & Modulus convention $k$ to $m$, that is $m = k^2$ \\
        \fmkey{m$\rightarrow$k}   & Modulus convention $m$ to $k$ \\
    \end{ntable}

    \shotfig{01-ellipt}{The Ellipt page, which is the whole menu: the unshifted row along the bottom,
    the f row above it, the g row above that.  The tiny S over L at the top right is the simulator's
    stack-lift indicator; hardware carries the battery gauge there.}{fig:ellipt}

    In each case except for $\Pi(n,m)$, the $u$ or $\phi$ parameter is the primary argument, and the
    $m$ parameter is the square of eccentricity of the ellipse.
    For $\Pi(n,m)$, $n$ is the characteristic and $m$ is the square of eccentricity.

